# C# guards and control-flow shape

One rule family: **guards first**, **no redundant braces** for single statements, **no trailing `else`** after a terminating branch.

---

## Same-line guard clauses

When an `if` body is a single guard statement (`throw …;`, `return;`, `return <expr>;`, `continue;`, `break;`), put it on the **same line** as the `if`, with no braces—including **`if (predicate) return result;`** when the **whole assembled line** is still readable (team tooling uses **≤ 160** characters for auto-collapse; shorten condition or hoist a local if it blows past that).

### Preferred

```csharp
if (key is null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(key));
if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(key)) return;
if (tenant is null) return null;
if (!TryParse(span, out int n)) return false;
if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(key)) continue;
if (cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested) return Task.CompletedTask;
```

### Avoid

```csharp
// Avoid — multiline guard when a single-line guard fits
if (key is null)
{
    throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(key));
}

// Avoid — put return on same line as if when it fits
if (!TryParse(span, out int n))
    return false;
```

### Scope

- Body must be **one** statement and must be a guard — `throw`, `return`, `continue`, `break`, `goto`.
- Keep **one blank line before** the `if` per the project blank-line rule, **except** when the guard is the very first statement of the member or block.
- See also **`CSharp-Members-And-Construction.mdc`** (never stack multiple blank lines).

---

## Early return — no trailing `else`

When an `if` branch ends in `return`, `throw`, `continue`, or `break`, **do not** wrap the rest of the method in an `else` block. Let control fall through.

### Preferred

```csharp
public string Resolve(Tenant tenant)
{
    if (tenant is null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(tenant));
    if (!tenant.IsActive) return "(inactive)";

    return Format(tenant);
}
```

```csharp
foreach (string key in keys)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(key)) continue;

    Process(key);
}
```

### Avoid

```csharp
// Avoid: trailing else after a return / throw
if (tenant is null)
{
    throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(tenant));
}
else if (!tenant.IsActive)
{
    return "(inactive)";
}
else
{
    return Format(tenant);
}
```

### Scope

- Stay with `if` / `else if` / `else` only when **every** branch produces a value at the same level (peer mappings) — then prefer **`CSharp-Terse-Modern-Language.mdc`** (switch expressions).

---

## Embedded statements: omit braces when one statement

When a control-flow construct has **exactly one** embedded statement and C# does **not** require a block, **do not** wrap that statement in `{ }`.

### Applies to

`if`, `else`, `while`, `for`, `foreach`, `lock`, and `using` where the body is a single statement.

### Do

Prefer **braceless**, and **prefer the same physical line** when condition + statement stay short:

```csharp
if (value is null) return;

foreach (string id in ids)
    Register(id);
```

Otherwise break across lines — still **without** `{}`:

```csharp
if (!TryParse(input, out int n))
    throw new InvalidOperationException("…");
```

Keep **one blank line before** `if` / `foreach` (etc.) when not the first statement in the member.

### Do not

Do not add braces solely for a single statement:

```csharp
// Avoid
if (x)
{
    return;
}
```

Use a **block** when the body is more than one statement, when you need scoped `locals`, or when omitting braces would confuse `else` binding or readability.

### Tooling

Repo **`.editorconfig`** sets `csharp_prefer_braces = false` (IDE0011). Apply **IDE0011-only** formatting:

`dotnet format ArchLucid.sln style --diagnostics IDE0011 --severity info`

See **`docs/library/FORMATTING.md`** for **`scripts/RemoveEmbeddedStatementBraces`**.

---

## Single embedded statements — one physical line when short

Aligns with **`csharp_prefer_braces = false`** (IDE0011).

When a construct has **exactly one** embedded statement (`if`, `else`, `for`, `foreach`, `while`, `using`, `lock`, …):

1. **Do not wrap** in `{ }` unless multi-statement, locals, or dangling-`else` safety requires a block (same as **Embedded statements** above).

2. **Prefer one physical line** for short **`if (...)` + body**. For guards, same-line as **Same-line guard clauses** (`if (cond) return;`, `if (cond) return expr;`).

3. **When the line would be overly long**, keep the `if (...)` on one logical line or break the condition; put the body on the next indented line **still without braces**.

   **Rough cap:** **≤ 160** characters for a single assembled guard line (`RemoveEmbeddedStatementBraces` uses 160).

**Blank lines:** One blank line before `if` / `foreach` when not the first statement in the member.

---

## Invert `if`s — flatten nesting with guard clauses

Prefer **guard clauses** and **early exits** over nesting the main logic inside positive branches. When a branch only handles an exceptional, empty, or short path (`return`, `throw`, `continue`, `break`), handle it first so the remainder stays at **minimal indentation**.

### When *not* to force inversion

Keep symmetric **`if` / `else if` / `else`** (or a **`switch` expression**) when **every** branch is a **peer** computation (mapping, routing, parsers) — use **`CSharp-Terse-Modern-Language.mdc`**.

---

## Single-line `throw` (no braces)

When an `if` (or `else if`) has **only** a `throw` as its body, **do not** wrap it in `{ }`.

**Preferred:**

```csharp
if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name))
    throw new ArgumentException(nameof(name));
```

Same-line is also fine when short: `if (x is null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(x));`

Does **not** relax: multi-statement branches, loops, and `catch` blocks still use braces. If analyzers conflict, follow the **stricter** gate.

---

## Single-line `continue` (no braces)

When a loop guard's body is **only** `continue;`, **do not** wrap in `{ }`. Prefer same-line as `if` when the condition is short.

```csharp
foreach (string key in keys)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(key)) continue;

    Process(key);
}
```

Two-line form when the condition is long. Multi-statement guards still use `{ }`.
